<article class="env-lemma" id="074U">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/074U">Lemma <span data-tag="074U">11.7.2</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a finite central simple algebra over $k$, and let $B$ be a simple subalgebra of $A$. If $B$ is a central $k$-algebra, then $A = B \otimes _ k C$ where $C$ is the (central simple) centralizer of $B$ in $A$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have $\dim _ k(A) = \dim _ k(B \otimes _ k C)$ by Theorem <a href="/tag/074T" data-tag="074T">11.7.1</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/074F" data-tag="074F">11.4.7</a> the tensor product is simple. Hence the natural map $B \otimes _ k C \to A$ is injective hence an isomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>